            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Symphonies</strong><br>\nMahler's main contribution to music is his symphonies. He wrote ten in total, with the last one left unfinished at his death. His works are characterized by their large scale and emotional depth. They often incorporated voices and literary themes, which was unusual for symphonies at the time. Mahler's symphonies continue to be a significant part of classical music repertoire worldwide.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Song Cycles</strong><br>\nAlongside his symphonies, Mahler composed several song cycles, such as \"Des Knaben Wunderhorn\" and \"Kindertotenlieder.\" These works are known for their beautiful melodies and profound expression. The text for these songs often came from German folk poetry and literature, reflecting Mahler's deep connection to his cultural roots and his love for storytelling through music.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mahler's Influence and Legacy</strong><br>\nMahler's influence extends far beyond his own compositions. He played a critical role in shaping the modern orchestra and had a lasting impact on the evolution of classical music. His innovations in symphony structure and orchestration have inspired generations of composers. Mahler's work laid the groundwork for many of the developments in 20th-century classical music.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
