          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Struggles and Triumphs</strong><br>\nMahler's life was marked by personal challenges, including the deaths of several family members and his own health issues. Despite these adversities, he continued to compose and conduct, often using his personal experiences as inspiration for his music. His perseverance in the face of struggle is reflected in the emotional intensity of his work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Reception and Criticism</strong><br>\nDuring his lifetime, Mahler's work received mixed reviews. Some criticized his compositions for being overly complex and philosophical. However, as time passed, appreciation for his music grew. Today, Mahler is celebrated for his unique contributions to music and for the powerful emotional experiences his works evoke in listeners.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mahler's Final Years</strong><br>\nMahler's final years were both creatively fruitful and personally difficult. He moved to America, where he led the New York Philharmonic and the Metropolitan Opera. Unfortunately, his health deteriorated, and he passed away on May 18, 1911. His music, once overshadowed by his conducting fame, gained posthumous recognition, securing his place as one of the great composers in history.</p>\n"
              },
